{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"api_error_status":null,"duration_ms":32370,"duration_api_ms":33065,"num_turns":11,"result":"I wrote the script to `src/49-cpufreq/cpufreq.bt`, but I haven't run it. I couldn't check whether `bpftrace` or the tracepoint exists here, because the shell command needed approval.\n\nIt attaches to `tracepoint:power:cpu_frequency`. For every frequency change it prints a line with the time, the CPU, and the old and new frequency in MHz. When you press Ctrl-C it prints three summaries:\n- **Transitions:** the number of frequency changes per CPU.\n- **Frequency histogram:** how often each frequency was set, in 100 MHz buckets.\n- **Residency:** the microseconds each CPU spent at each frequency, counted from one change to the next.\n\nRun it with `sudo bpftrace src/49-cpufreq/cpufreq.bt`.\n\nThe tracepoint only fires for cpufreq drivers that report frequency transitions, such as `acpi-cpufreq`, `intel_pstate` in passive mode, and `amd-pstate`. Some drivers in active mode don't report them, and the script will print nothing on those machines.\n\nI picked the directory name `49-cpufreq` myself, since 48 was already taken by `48-energy`. Tell me if you'd like it elsewhere, or want a README and Makefile to match the other examples.","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"d59f7269-fa4c-4821-b47b-b17e58e15b95","total_cost_usd":0.1167106,"usage":{"input_tokens":18,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3538,"cache_read_input_tokens":209977,"output_tokens":2663,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3538},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":432,"cache_read_input_tokens":27211,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1605,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1605,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputToke